      "id": 1,
      "text": "The historic Concord Missionary Baptist Church was organized on April 21, 1855 by pioneer settlers before the town itself was founded.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historictexas.net/city/concord-texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 2011 a Labor Day weekend wildfire destroyed the Concord Missionary Baptist Church sanctuary, but a marker now commemorates the site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historictexas.net/city/concord-texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1900 Concord had become a railroad switch on the International‑Great Northern line, linking the community to regional trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/concord-tx-mclennan-county",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Concord sits five miles northeast of Waco on the old Corsicana Road, a historic route that once carried early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/concord-tx-mclennan-county",
